{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1474910976,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDgwMjYzNDg0MA=="},"added":["conference agenda item"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1474916772,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI0OTY2NjE5NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921#issuecomment-249666195"},"message":"I am not sure whether we need to classify different reasons for not publishing pieces. We could simply have **donotpublish**. If we want to classify, I'd prefer to have general labels like **sensitive**, rather than _non-defaced_.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1474918137,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI0OTY3MjExMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921#issuecomment-249672113"},"message":"we don't need to but I think it would be better to classify so we ourselves (and others) know why specific file shouldn't be redistributed without consideration.  May be with some collaborators I would like to share one type of data (e.g. \"sensitive\") but not the other (e.g. \"proprietary\"), which then could be easily done if we separate those out.  And it is still as easy to say \"with any restriction\"","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1474918180,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI0OTY3MjI3Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921#issuecomment-249672272"},"message":"True.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1474970185,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI0OTgyMDI0MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921#issuecomment-249820240"},"message":"Do you see a way to let the users specify the classification terms while `datalad` is just providing the mechanism to allow for such definitions? Depending on the field the data is about it might heavily differ what terms really make sense. And we don't want to have it convenient for neuroscientists, while everybody else has to use **sensitive** for everything, do we?\nOtherwise I agree.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1474970509,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI0OTgyMTQ0MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921#issuecomment-249821441"},"message":"Actually, we can use any tag to specify `redistribution-restrictions`, right? So we probably need just a proxy command to assign tags (recursively).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1474972817,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI0OTgyOTM2OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921#issuecomment-249829368"},"message":"Yes, we need helpers for metadata. Be careful -  Annex metadata has tags (entries without values) and actual key=value entries. And those are somewhat different I think ;-)","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1475160670,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE0OlVubGFiZWxlZEV2ZW50ODA3MTI0Nzg1"},"added":[],"removed":["conference agenda item"]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1475160851,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI1MDQ5MDI2OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921#issuecomment-250490268"},"message":"a sample case was http://datasets.datalad.org/?dir=/labs/haxby/raiders and it worked out nicely","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1475160879,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDgwNzEzMDg4NA=="},"added":["documentation"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1487052870,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI3OTYxNzg0MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921#issuecomment-279617840"},"message":"Looking into this again, I think we should not diversify tags with the same intended consequences too much. In order to have publish not publish content with restrictions we need to set up default preferred content expressions. Those are per local remote, hence cannot be informed by the original dataset creator. The only way I see this work is to have the creator use a defined meta data tag for labeling restricted content, and have `create-sibling` always set up a preferred content expression that prevents such data from going to a remote, unless explicitly instructed that it is OK to accept restricted content.\n\nMinimal first steps from my PoV:\n\n- [ ] The `crawler` needs to start marking all file content that is from behind an auth-wall with a tag (maybe `auth-required`, little more specific than just 'authentication').\n- [ ] `create-sibling` needs to get a switch to override publication restrictions (which need to be ON be default, in order to avoid accidental publication), for example in the context of a \"backup\" remote (#925)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1487088288,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI3OTc1MDY2Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921#issuecomment-279750663"},"message":"1. agreed that crawler should mark all restricted content!  I was just waiting for us to reach conclusion to define precisely names and values for the meta-data to be used. So far I think I have used only `distribution-restrictions=sensitive` (and `distribution-restrictions=proprietary`), so if no objections, could add `auto-required`, so it could then look like (just an example on raiders ds):\n```shell\nhopa:/tmp/raiders/sub001/anatomy\n$\u003e git annex metadata --set distribution-restrictions+=auth-required highres004.PAR\nmetadata highres004.PAR \n  dcterms_format=image/parrec\n  dcterms_format-lastchanged=2016-09-26@19-37-46\n  distribution-restrictions=auth-required\n  distribution-restrictions=sensitive\n  distribution-restrictions-lastchanged=2017-02-14@15-59-46\n  lastchanged=2017-02-14@15-59-46\nok\n(recording state in git...)\n```\n\n2. `create-sibling` indeed should be capable to specify pretty much either some standard annex group (https://git-annex.branchable.com/preferred_content/standard_groups/) or some custom ones we add (e.g. to not publish anything with restrictions).  But I think that by default it should either do nothing, or set it to `manual` group.  Then `publish` by default would copy no data, and would copy to remote host data only if either explicit PATH is provided, or no PATHs provided but some annex wanted setting/group is specified so `annex copy --auto` does its job (see https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1274#issuecomment-279747603 for more detail/analysis)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1487092282,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI3OTc3MTE0NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921#issuecomment-279771145"},"message":"Re 2: Not sure, whether I fully understand your wishes for `publish`, @yarikoptic .\nDo you want `publish some/path` to ignore those restrictions without any switch explicitly saying to do so?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1569072171,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzMzc5NzcxNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921#issuecomment-533797717"},"message":"\u003e Do you want `publish some/path` to ignore those restrictions without any switch explicitly saying to do so?\n\nthat is a good question.  Current behavior -- yes, AFAIK we effectively ignore those `wanted` settings whenever specific paths are provided to `publish` since they are in effect only for `annex copy --auto` which we invoke **only** when no paths are provided.  We might want to reconsider and see how to fix that.","files":null}]}